    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.files\n\n### Neovim version\n0.11.x\n\n### Description\nWhen a file is renamed to a path that ends with a `/`, it should be treated as an instruction to replace the file with a directory at that location.\n\n### Example\n`test.txt` is renamed to `test/`\n\n**Expected behavior:**\n\n- `test.txt` is removed.\n\n- A new directory named `test/` is created.\n\n**Current behavior:**\n\n- `test.txt` is renamed to `test` (still a file)\n\n### Reasoning\nI usually forget to add a trailing `/` when I intend to create a directory. Instead of restarting and deleting the file I just created, I find this workflow much more simple.\n\n### Next Steps\nI have a working implementation for this and can open a PR if the feedback here is positive.",
